        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Huế</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 300 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">The oldest ruins in the area belong to the Kingdom of Lâm Ấp and date to the 4th century CE; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a> based themselves at Phú Xuân in 1687, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyễn Ánh</a> made it the imperial capital after 1802.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">On the Perfume River (Sông Hương) in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, between mountains, hills, plains and a coastal lagoon, bordering Quảng Trị to the north, Đà Nẵng to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> to the east.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> ruins of Kandarpapura; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuan-hoa">Thuận Hóa</a>, ceded to Đại Việt in 1306; Phú Xuân, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a>; the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> under the Nguyễn dynasty, 1802 to 1945; the Imperial City and imperial tombs; the áo dài; Buddhist monasteries and Huế cuisine</span></div>
