    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuan-hoa">Thuận Hóa</a> region passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> to Đại Việt in 1306, and from the late 16th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a> governed it and based themselves at Phú Xuân in 1687.</li><li>Phú Xuân was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Đàng Trong</a> from 1738 to 1775, fell to the Trịnh lords in 1775 and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> in 1786, and was retaken in 1802 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyễn Ánh</a>, who made it the capital of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</li><li>It was renamed Huế in 1899 under the French, remained the seat of the Nguyễn emperors until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a> abdicated in 1945, and became a centrally governed city in 2025.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first written use of the name Huế may be a line of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-nom">Chữ Nôm</a> poem attributed to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-thanh-tong">Lê Thánh Tông</a> that mentions &quot;Huế <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>&quot;; older documents called the place Phú Xuân, Kinh Đô or simply Kinh.</p></aside>
    </section>

